>>> like it was charred or obviously leaking.  When I powered it on there
>>> were some tiny sparks on the edge of a trace (which wasn't connected
>>> to anything at that point) and on the edge of a solder point of a
>>> diode.  Both things were near the pair of 200v1200uF capacitors...but
>>> there are other things near there as well.
>> Find a local TV repair shop and take it into them... anybody who can
>> repair those damned flat panel supplies should have no problem at all
>> fixing one of these.
>> --scott
>>
>> --
>> "C'est un Nagra. C'est suisse, et tres, tres precis."
> 
> That's not a bad idea.  Thanks!

Even if you know hardware, they can be tricky to fix. If the fuse blows, 
usual
culprits are input bridge rectifier or main power transistor short, but 
other
parts can get taken out as well, particularly switching diodes around the hv
section. Safest way is to replace all diodes and transistors in the hv 
section
and also check no resistors are open circuit and / or caps shorted.

On all kit which hasn't run for years, it's a good thing to bring the mains
voltage up slowly using a variac or similar, to reform the electrolytic 
caps.
They can fail / catch fire / explode if full line volts is suddenly 
applied.
If you don't have a variac, a 100w mains filament lamp in series with 
the line,
can be used to limit the current...

Regards,

Chris


