Christian Corti <use@reply.to> wrote:
> ChrisQ <meru@devnull.com> wrote:
>> On all kit which hasn't run for years, it's a good thing to 
>> bring the mains voltage up slowly using a variac or similar, 
>> to reform the electrolytic caps.

> Wrong, wrong!!! Never do that with a SMPS, especially with older ones
> (80s and earlier)!

Well, the first part of the usual power supply is either a voltage
doubler or bridge rectifier into two big capacitors at about 300VDC.

You should be able to reform those without any problem, if you
disconnect them from the rest of the circuit.  

Then there are filter capacitors at the end of each different
voltage.  For them, starting slow doesn't help much.  If you 
worry about them, you can take them out and reform each separately.

> And BTW, you don't need to reform any caps in such modern equipment
> (post-70s).

The previous poster suggested either variac or a current limiting
light bulb in series.  The latter for a few seconds shouldn't hurt
it, and will allow the capacitors a little more time to reform
than the sudden surge at full voltage.  

I probably wouldn't worry so much for later years equipment,
but then you never know about the quality of the original parts.

-- glen


