Christian Corti  <cc@corti-net.de> wrote:
>ChrisQ <meru@devnull.com> wrote:
>> On all kit which hasn't run for years, it's a good thing to bring the mains
>> voltage up slowly using a variac or similar, to reform the electrolytic 
>> caps.
>
>Wrong, wrong!!! Never do that with a SMPS, especially with older ones
>(80s and earlier)!
>And BTW, you don't need to reform any caps in such modern equipment
>(post-70s).

Well, modern caps still can get deformed if they are sitting on the shelf with
no charge across them.

But the variac won't help you with them with a switching supply.  You can
charge the big caps on the primary up slowly, but the thing won't start
to oscillate until it's able to produce full voltage on the secondaries.

The variac IS a good tool to bring the supply up on the bench slowly when
something is happening like the switching transistor blowing up.  Either
that, or a light bulb panel that provides some amount of current limiting,
so that after you have replaced the bad transistor you can bring it up
without blowing the new one, so you can figure out what blew it.
--scott

