glen herrmannsfeldt  <gah@ugcs.caltech.edu> wrote:
>
>The previous poster suggested either variac or a current limiting
>light bulb in series.  The latter for a few seconds shouldn't hurt
>it, and will allow the capacitors a little more time to reform
>than the sudden surge at full voltage.  
>
>I probably wouldn't worry so much for later years equipment,
>but then you never know about the quality of the original parts.

DEC was very good about using long-life high temperature electrolytics,
and capacitor failures are relatively rare compared with consumer gear.
I've occasionally replaced caps in VT220s and VT240s after they'd been
running continuously for a couple decades, but the need to just shotgun
out everything (as is common for consumer gear of that era) is absent.

Mind you, DEC gear still has the same dry-slug tantalum failures that
everyone else does....
--scott

