Christian Corti wrote:
> ChrisQ <meru@devnull.com> wrote:
>> On all kit which hasn't run for years, it's a good thing to bring the mains
>> voltage up slowly using a variac or similar, to reform the electrolytic 
>> caps.
> 
> Wrong, wrong!!! Never do that with a SMPS, especially with older ones
> (80s and earlier)!
> And BTW, you don't need to reform any caps in such modern equipment
> (post-70s).
> 
> Christian
> 

Speaking from experience, having repaired switch mode supplies since the 
mid 80's
from time to time, I always bring up line voltage slowly on kit that 
hasn't been
run for a number of years. This after several examples of caps either 
smoking or blowing
the sleeve off and depositing foil and paper everywhere. The low voltage 
end is not
so critical, though those caps do tend to dry out with temperature / age 
and go high Z,
with lowered or even near zero measured capacitance in some cases.

The op described symptoms, "smell of old tyres", is almost certainly 
one or more
of the input reservoir caps popping due to excessive leakage current, 
which may have been
avoided by bringing up the voltage slowly. It's unlikely to do any 
damage to the
rest of the circuit. Imho, modern kit is no better, if it hasn't been 
powered up for
many years. If anything, the older kit was far more conservatively 
engineered...

Regards,

Chris




